{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 60,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physics is the business of figuring out \nthe structure of the world. So are our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4740,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brains. But sometimes physics comes to \nconclusions that are in direct conflict  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8940,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with concepts fundamental to our minds, \nsuch as the realness of space and time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14940,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do we tell who’s correct? Are time and space \nobjective realities or human-invented concepts?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27900,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s been quite a few surprises in the history \nof the development of physics. And now we may be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at one of those special junctions again—many \nphysicists suspect that the next step will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38760,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pull the rug out from under us by showing \nthat space and time are not quite real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43740,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do we prepare for such a massive frameshift? \nTo be fair, our ideas about space and time have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50700,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not always been the same. In a previous episode \nwe talked about the two radically opposing ideas:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57120,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the “Absolute” space and time view, championed \nby Newton, space exists on its own, with no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64260,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regard to any objects or entities, and time also \nexists, its passage governed by a cosmic clock.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70680,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the time, this was a hefty proposition. \nMany philosophers and scientists, such as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76980,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leibnitz and Descarte considered \nspace and time as “relational”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81540,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a network of distances between \nobjects or succession of events.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87600,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, we saw that Newton’s pure vision \nof absolute space and time couldn’t be right.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94920,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if the dimensions don’t ultimately “look \nlike” our impression of them, what are they? Does  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101700,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our mental experience of space and time resemble \nthe world external to our subjective experience?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108840,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton clearly thought that there must be a very \nclose correspondence. Leibnitz on the other hand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114660,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thought that we build our experience of space \nthrough distilling positional relations that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121260,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are inherent to the connections between objects, \nrather than space being a standalone container  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126120,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for those objects. Another prodigious thinker \nwho thought similarly was Immanual Kant. He  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132300,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "initially took Newton’s side on the reality \nof space and time, but after what he calls  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137640,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a Copernican revolution in his thinking, he came \nto believe that space and time are not physically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142980,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real but are constructs of the mind--inborn \nprinciples by which we organize the world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149040,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last time we turned to Einstein as the ultimate \ntie breaker. In his essay about “the problem of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155940,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space”, Einstein wrote that “concepts of space and \ntime are free creations of the human intelligence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162060,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tools of thought, which are to serve \nthe purpose of bringing experiences into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167460,
    "dDurationMs": 8100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relation with each other.” Uh. OK, so we don’t \njust believe Einstein because he’s Einstein.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175560,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we sure don’t dismiss something Albert \nsays without thinking very hard about it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, are space and time, absolute and \nfundamental, or relational and conceptual?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187140,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There might be new clues in the mechanisms by \nwhich brains manage space and time, so today we’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to do something unusual. We’re going to do \nsome neuroscience to try to understand how these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimensions are dealt with in the brain and what \nthat implies about their existence in the world.."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205080,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok. To be spatially capable organisms, we \nneed our brain to do two things. Tell us 1)  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212700,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where things are in relation to us, and 2) Where \neverything is in relation to each other in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220260,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The latter, the allocentric map of space, is more \nrelevant to our discussion so we’ll focus on that.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228180,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To navigate the environment, brains seem \nto generate a mental representation of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233640,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surrounding environment. This \nis often called a ‘cognitive map’."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237060,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1971, neuroscientists John O'Keefe \nand Jonothon Dostrovsky found the first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence for this map in the brain. They \nwere monitoring neural activity in rats  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249000,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as they wandered around their environments. \nThey noticed that a neuron in a section of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254100,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rat’s hippocampus would fire rapidly every \ntime the rat entered a particular region."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259020,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And when the rat moved a bit further,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261240,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a different neuron would fire, and \nso on. They’d discovered place cells,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266400,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and some have argued that they represent \nour internal map. In a given environment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271260,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "specific place cells correspond to specific \nlocations, and those neurons remain fixed on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277020,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those locations until you go to a new environment. \nThen, the place cells remap to the new space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283620,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, how do these cells know where the rat is?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287040,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2005 Edvard and May-britt Moser discovered \nanother group of neurons that behaved a little  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293100,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the place cells. These are in an area \nnext to the hippocampus—the entorhinal cortex.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299580,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They also fire when an experimental rat \npasses through a particular location in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304800,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their physical environment. But that \nsame neuron will also fire when the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309480,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rat enters any number of other locations at \nequal distances from each other—locations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314580,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that together form a hexagonal grid \nspanning the current environment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319380,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the grid cells, and they \nappear to tile whatever space we’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325200,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "currently in into grids of multiple \ndifferent orientations and scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330180,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although each new space is tiled \ndifferently, individual grid cells  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334500,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "represent a fixed scale--they are like \na rigid ruler providing something like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339780,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metric information with different resolutions. \nImagine how this plays out in your own head.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346440,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’re walking across a large room and a given \nneuron in your entorhinal cortex clicks every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353040,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3 meters. A different neuron fires more often \n- approximately every meter - while another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358140,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is clocking your progress every 10 meters, \nand so on. Any location in the space elicits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364620,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a unique pattern of “firing grid cells” which \nin turn leads to the firing of a unique place  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370860,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cell. One view is that the brain is doing \nan inverse fourier transform, through which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376740,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a combination of signals from multiple different \ngrid cells stack to excite localized place cells."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383280,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now there are a lot of unknowns here,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385440,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and different interpretations about what all \nthese cells are doing, which we’ll come back to.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390420,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regardless, the combination of grid cells and \nplace cells seem to be a key part of the machinery  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396600,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind your sense of your surrounding space. And \nit has some very telling properties. It seems like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403500,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it includes a coordinate grid. In that sense, \nit’s a distinctly Newtonian picture of space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409440,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with coordinates fixed to the current environment \nand independent of your location within it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414780,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also independent of things in that \nenvironment. This makes it feel absolute  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419580,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than relational. Perhaps you remember \nthat in the relational view, space is thought  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424860,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of as a network of distance relationships between \nobjects, and that’s not what grid cells represent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430500,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is only the tip of the \niceberg of our spatial processing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434400,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "machinery. We talked about our \nallocentric modeling of space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438060,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which appears absolute. But we also perform \negocentric processing—understanding the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surrounding space in context of ourselves, \nand this is by definition relational."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448440,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We use depth perception to help construct \nthe grid, and we use our internal sense of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our velocity and direction of motion \nto update our position on the grid.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These relational distance measures help \nus build and update a sense of space that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463920,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is absolute and not centered \non ourselves or anything else."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467580,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our hippocampus and entorhinal systems seem \nhard-wired to represent space the way they do,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473580,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so perhaps it’s understandable why \nNewton—and now many of us—have developed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478320,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this intuition of space as an absolute \nentity that’s independent of its contents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483900,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We haven’t yet got to the big question of whether \nthe space in our heads is just in our heads,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490680,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although we already have some useful clues. \nBefore we get to the really hard question,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495780,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need to spend a little time on … time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499260,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, Newton thought that time was as absolute \nas space, ticking away everywhere at the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506280,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate even absent anything to experience it. Einstein showed that the rate of clocks depends on motion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and gravity, but also hinted that he didn’t \nbuy the idea of time as independent of things  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that experience time. He said that “Time is what \nclocks measure.” suggesting that time isn’t an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526320,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absolute thing in itself, but rather something \nthat emerges from the behavior of matter.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531120,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked about the origin of time in this \nsense before. Any thing that undergoes internal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536520,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "change can be thought of as a clock. For a good \nclock, that change is regular. Perhaps periodic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543780,
    "dDurationMs": 7401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the swinging of a pendulum or measured by \naccumulated change, like the sand in an hourglass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551181,
    "dDurationMs": 6699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, we have no “time receptors,” in our brains, as we do for other features of perception, like color,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557880,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pitch. But we know that our brains can tell \ntime. We wake and sleep on a regular cycle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563940,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can guess when a certain amount of \ntime has passed with varying accuracy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568260,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can sync up our limbs for coordinated \nmovement, and we can keep a beat—or at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574080,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "least some of us can. We must have an \ninternal clock of some sort. Although  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579060,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually it’s no longer generally believed \nthat we have a single master clock. Rather,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584280,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different brain regions probably find their \nown ways to model or track the passage of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589260,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One top candidate is the rhythmic activity created \nby populations of neurons firing in sync. These  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596220,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brain waves repeat on a regular timescale, \nfrom 0.02 to 600 cycles per second (Hz),  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603120,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and some of them might be creating something \nlike a ticking clock that other neurons can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608520,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "organize themselves around. We also track time \non different scales through our circadian rhythm,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615600,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the accumulation and ordering of our \nmemories, and through other neural methods."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620220,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of these inner clocks track experienced time, \nand are only loosely correlated with external  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626160,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time. On very short timescales our timing is \nrather good and consistent—we coordinate our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631860,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limbs and our senses, and we’re good at guessing \nhow much time has passed up to several seconds.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637440,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But our guesses get worse for longer time \nperiods, and our sense of time is warped by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642540,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whatever’s going on—time flies when you’re \nhaving fun, but a watched pot never boils."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648540,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, our time perception does not seem \nabsolute. It seems distinctly relational and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654360,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very malleable—we remember temporal ordering of \nevents and estimate intervals between events.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661140,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we don’t have an inbuilt proxy \nfor Newton’s singular cosmic clock.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666060,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This may be why we wear a watch but \ndon’t carry personal yardsticks!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670260,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we’ve talked about how the brain \nmodels space and time. Things get really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675840,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interesting when we bring these together. \nIn fact the brain’s distinction between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and time is not so cut and dry. For \nexample, researchers have seen that under  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certain conditions hippocampal cells seem to \ntrack progression of time rather than place.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They fire sequentially as time passes \nfor a rat running in place in a wheel.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696360,
    "dDurationMs": 7445,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it now seems that place cells can fire \nwith new locations OR with the passage of time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703805,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They may even have a much more general \nfunction, and that’s tracking sequences."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709325,
    "dDurationMs": 5695,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I already talked about brain rhythms as \ninternal coordinating clocks. There is one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715020,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such brain wave in the hippocampus: the theta \ncycle, periodic neuronal pulsing at 4-10 Hz."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721200,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On every pulse of the theta cycle, a chain of \nplace cells will fire in rapid succession—in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the middle is the neuron representing \nthe current location or time. But first,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733440,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a group of neurons representing the \nrecent past will fire in sequence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738840,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then a sequence representing the upcoming \nplaces in the trajectory. On each theta wave, you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745980,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "live partially in your immediate past and future. \nThis suggests that place cells may ultimately  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752760,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reflect your executed and planned trajectory \nrather than specifically space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759920,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This trajectory may be in actual space, or \nin an abstract space of thought and intention,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767520,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or even a logical chain of reasoning. Evidence \nin favor of this is that we know that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774180,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hippocampus is critical for laying down memories. \nIt indexes past experiences in a way that enables  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us to recall them in sequence—almost as though \nit was applying a coordinate system to them.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787440,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems that the machinery that may initially \nhave evolved for enabling navigation through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792780,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space has been co-opted into a much bigger \nrole. This is also true of the grid cells,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798000,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which seem to play a role in mapping 2-dimensional \nabstract spaces. For example, they’re active  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when we build mental models that involve the \nrelationships between pairs of related variables.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809880,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We seem to use grid cells and place cells to \nhelp us organize the world in very general ways,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and navigation in real space and in mental \nspace could be using the same algorithm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s regroup. We started all of this \nby asking if the space and time of our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828000,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minds corresponds to physically real entities.\nSome pretty smart people, including Einstein,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834360,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thought that perception of space and time are \nmental constructs. Now let me be very clear;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they were not saying that the external world \nisn’t real. They believed that there’s something  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845760,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out there that has an independent existence \nto us. That something exhibits regularities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851460,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that our brains partition into space and \ntime. Leibnitz, Kant, and Einstein felt  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857880,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that those regularities only take on our familiar \nexperience of space and time within our minds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864060,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So does the neuroscience we learned agree with \nthem? We can’t answer that directly but we can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871740,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "try to say whether our brains are capable of such \na feat. And the answer to that looks to be a yes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many researchers believe that our mechanisms \nfor tracking time and space are really general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883800,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "purpose algorithms for tracking sequences of \nevents and mapping the relationships between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888900,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "continuous variables. And with navigation \nbeing such an essential function for survival,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895200,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it seems likely that these systems evolved with \nthe original purpose of doing this job for space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901980,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and time. John O’keefe, discoverer of place \ncells put it this way: let’s assume that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907800,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world is an n-dimensional energy soup. Animals \non all levels of the evolutionary scale develop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913980,
    "dDurationMs": 6579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems sensitive to various aspects of this \nsoup; these become their version of reality.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920559,
    "dDurationMs": 5541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One evolutionary development led to a set of \nsystems which divided the soup sharply into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926100,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discrete objects and provided a spatial \nframework for containing these objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932160,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, arranging the world into what and where and when is our brain’s most efficient and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938220,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaningful way of carving nature at its joints. \nThe fundamentality and primacy of space and time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945420,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may stem from the fact that we have no alternative \nway of partitioning our experience. Many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951660,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientists are accepting the demise of spacetime \nas a fundamental entity. In future episodes in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958920,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this series we’ll get back to the implications of \nthis in physics.What fundamental structures and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964980,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "processes give rise to external regularities \nthat our brain represents as spacetime?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
